{
  "title": "Roman To Integer",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "type": "array",
  "tags": [
    "Hash Table",
    "String"
  ],
  "description": "Converts a Roman numeral string (e.g., 'IV', 'MCMXCIV') to its integer equivalent by iterating through the characters and handling subtractive cases (like 'IV' for 4) by checking the next character.",
  "pseudocode": "1. function romanToInt(s):\n2.   map = {'I': 1, 'V': 5, 'X': 10, 'L': 50, 'C': 100, 'D': 500, 'M': 1000}\n3.   total = 0\n4.   i = 0\n5.   while i < length(s):\n6.     currentValue = map[s[i]]\n7.     // Check if there is a next character\n8.     if i + 1 < length(s):\n9.       nextValue = map[s[i+1]]\n10.      // If current value is smaller than next, it's a subtractive case (e.g., IV, IX)\n11.      if currentValue < nextValue:\n12.        total = total - currentValue\n13.      else:\n14.        total = total + currentValue\n15.    else: // Last character, always add its value\n16.      total = total + currentValue\n17.    i = i + 1\n18.  return total",
